**user**:
> I have a contract with a solar contractor, but they haven't finished the project and it's been almost a year. Is there a point at which I can say they are in breach of contract, or otherwise force them to do the work? The contract has an "estimated 6 months" mentioned in it, but that's all.
**assistant**:
> You’re bumping up against contract-law questions where the details really matter, so I’ll outline the key ideas and then suggest concrete next steps you can take *before* paying a lawyer for a full review.
> 
> I’m not a lawyer and this isn’t legal advice, but this is how this usually works in U.S./Common Law–type jurisdictions.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 1. Does “estimated 6 months” actually bind them?
> 
> In many contracts, language like “estimated,” “approximate,” or “target” timeframe is *not* a firm deadline by itself. Courts often treat it as:
> 
> - A **non-binding estimate**, *unless*:
>   - There’s other language that makes timing “of the essence,” or
>   - The delay becomes so long that it’s unreasonable under the circumstances.
> 
> Key questions in your contract:
> 
> - Does it say anything like **“time is of the essence”**?
> - Are there **specific milestone dates** (e.g., “installation to be completed by X”)?
> - Are there **permitting or interconnection contingencies** (e.g., timelines dependent on utility approvals, permits, supply availability)?
> 
> If it only says “estimated 6 months” with no strong language around it, you probably can’t just say, “6 months passed, automatic breach.” But nearly a year for a residential or small commercial install *can* be plausibly argued as **unreasonable delay**, depending on context.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. When does delay become breach?
> 
> Even without a fixed date, contractors have a duty to perform **within a “reasonable time.”** What’s “reasonable” hinges on:
> 
> - Ordinary timelines for similar solar projects in your area
> - External constraints: permits, inspections, utility interconnection, equipment lead times
> - Whether they’ve been **communicative and actively working** vs. simply disappearing
> - Whether *you* have done everything required of you (payments, access to property, signatures for utility, etc.)
> 
> If they have:
> - Stopped responding,
> - Failed to schedule remaining work,
> - Or repeatedly promised dates and then done nothing,
> 
> then they are heading into **material breach** territory. You usually can’t just *declare* that on your own and walk away without some process, but you can start setting things up correctly.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. What you can do now (short of suing)
> 
> ### Step 1: Gather facts and paper trail
> 
> Collect:
> - The **signed contract** (PDF/scan).
> - All **emails / texts / portal messages** about:
>   - Schedule estimates,
>   - Reasons for delays,
>   - Promises to come back and finish.
> - Records of **payments** made and what was completed at each stage.
> - Any **permits / inspection results** or utility interconnection status.
> 
> This will matter both for negotiation and any legal review.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### Step 2: Ask them (in writing) for a clear completion plan
> 
> Before you go “legal,” send a calm, clear email. Something like:
> 
> > Subject: Request for Completion Timeline – [Project Address]
> >
> > Dear [Contractor],
> >
> > Our solar installation contract dated [date] indicates an estimated completion timeframe of six months. We are now approaching [X] months since signing, and the system is still not completed/operational.
> >
> > Please provide, in writing by [date 7–10 days from now], a firm schedule for:
> > 1. Remaining work to be performed,
> > 2. Target dates for inspection/utility approval (if applicable),
> > 3. Expected final commissioning date.
> >
> > We need to understand whether and when the project will be completed so that we can plan accordingly.
> >
> > Thank you,
> > [Your Name]
> 
> This:
> - Gives them fair notice,
> - Documents that you are trying to resolve this reasonably.
> 
> If they respond with a clear plan and then actually follow it, good. If they don’t:
> 
> ---
> 
> ### Step 3: “Notice to cure” / “demand letter”
> 
> If you’re ready to push harder, the next step is a more formal written notice that they are in **potential breach** and must fix it within a defined window.
> 
> Even without a lawyer, you can send something like (adapt to your situation):
> 
> > Subject: Formal Notice of Delay and Demand for Completion – [Project Address]
> >
> > Dear [Contractor],
> >
> > I am writing regarding our solar installation agreement dated [date]. The contract contemplated an estimated completion timeframe of approximately six months. As of today, [date], it has been nearly [X] months, and the project remains incomplete: [briefly list what is incomplete].
> >
> > I have previously requested an updated schedule (see my messages dated [dates]) but the project has not been completed and no firm timeline has been adhered to.
> >
> > This extended delay appears to constitute a failure to perform the contract within a reasonable time. I am hereby providing formal notice that unless:
> >
> > - Substantial progress is made and
> > - The project is fully completed and operational by [specific date, e.g., 30 days from now],
> >
> > I will consider you to be in material breach of the agreement and will pursue available remedies, which may include cancellation of the contract, hiring another contractor to complete the work, and seeking recovery of any additional costs, as well as filing complaints with [state licensing board / consumer protection agency / utility program administrator, if applicable].
> >
> > I prefer to resolve this amicably and would be happy to discuss any genuine obstacles you are facing, but I do need a completed and operational system by the above date.
> >
> > Sincerely,
> > [Your Name]  
> > [Address]  
> > [Phone/Email]
> 
> Send by:
> - Email **and**
> - Certified mail / something trackable, if possible.
> 
> This is often enough to shake a contractor into action, particularly if they’re just deprioritizing you.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 4. Leverage beyond the contract itself
> 
> Depending on where you are, you may have leverage via:
> 
> - **State contractor licensing board**  
>   - You can check whether they’re licensed and file a complaint.
> - **State or local consumer protection office / attorney general**  
>   - They often help with home improvement disputes, esp. if there’s a pattern.
> - **Solar incentive program administrators** (e.g., state solar rebate, SREC programs, or utility incentive programs)  
>   - If the contractor depends on program participation, a complaint can matter.
> - **Financing company or lender** (if you financed the system)  
>   - The lender may pressure them, especially if funds were disbursed but work is incomplete.
> 
> These channels can often move things without you personally litigating.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 5. When to talk to a lawyer
> 
> Talking to a lawyer is most useful if:
> 
> - You’ve sent a clear written demand with a deadline, and:
>   - They ignore it, or
>   - They give weak excuses and still don’t perform.
> - You’ve already paid most/all of the money.
> - You’re considering:
>   - **Terminating** the contract,
>   - **Hiring a new installer**, and
>   - **Trying to recover extra costs** from the original contractor.
> 
> What a lawyer will do:
> - Read the contract for cancellation/termination clauses.
> - Assess whether the delay is likely to be seen as a **material breach** in your jurisdiction.
> - Help you send a properly worded **demand** or **notice to cure** that preserves your rights.
> - Advise on whether you can safely cancel and move to another contractor.
> 
> It doesn’t have to be huge or expensive; sometimes a single consult and one attorney letter makes them suddenly take you very seriously.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 6. Practical tip about switching contractors
> 
> If you eventually decide to hire someone else:
> 
> - Get from the original contractor:
>   - As-built plans, engineering documents, equipment serial numbers, permit applications, and any interconnection paperwork already started.
> - Confirm with the **utility** and **permitting authority** that:
>   - The project can be reassigned to another installer,
>   - There aren’t any issues with duplicate applications or program rules.
> 
> ---
> 
